{#
  What a link to this page looks like when somebody shares it.

  Open Graph is read by everything that draws a card — Discord, Facebook,
  WhatsApp, Signal, Slack, iMessage — and X reads its own `twitter:` names
  first, falling back to the same Open Graph tags for anything it does not
  find. So the pair below is not a duplicate: it is one set of facts, with the
  three X insists on stated in its dialect.

  Every URL here is absolute, because a card is fetched by a machine that has
  no page to resolve a relative path against. That makes `site.url` load-
  bearing: get it wrong and every card on the site points at a host that is not
  serving it.
#}
{%- set pageUrl = site.url + (page.url | basePath) -%}
{%- set cardImage = site.url + (gameSlug | socialCard) -%}
{%- set cardDescription = pageDescription or subhead or (game.meta.description if game) or site.description -%}

{#
  The page's own name, without the site's.

  The <title> ends in "· Cardboard Appendix" because a browser tab is a row of
  unlabelled strips and the reader needs to know which site they are looking
  at. A card already says so on its own line, from `og:site_name`, so repeating
  it here would spend the headline on it twice.
#}
{%- set cardTitle -%}
  {%- if not title -%}
    {{ site.title }}
  {%- elif game and contentTypeSlug != "landing" -%}
    {{ title }} · {{ game.title }}
  {%- else -%}
    {{ title }}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endset -%}

<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ site.title }}">
<meta property="og:type" content="{% if page.url == '/' %}website{% else %}article{% endif %}">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ pageUrl }}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ cardTitle | trim }}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ cardDescription }}">
<meta property="og:locale" content="{{ site.lang }}">

<meta property="og:image" content="{{ cardImage }}">
{#
  Stated rather than left to be inferred: several platforms lay the card out
  before the image has finished arriving, and without these the text jumps when
  it lands. They are the card's own proportions, which `lib/og-cards.js` draws
  every image to.
#}
<meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
<meta property="og:image:height" content="630">
<meta property="og:image:alt" content="{% if game %}Box art for {{ game.title }}{% else %}{{ site.title }}{% endif %}">

<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
<meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ cardTitle | trim }}">
<meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ cardDescription }}">
<meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ cardImage }}">
{% if noIndex %}
  {#
    A page that is nobody's but this reader's, or that is not ready to be
    found. `noindex` keeps it out of results; it is not a lock, and the page is
    still served to anyone with the URL — which is the point of `unlisted`.
  #}
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow">
{% endif %}
